{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And if she should be to a man and her vows are upon her</b> — According to the opinion of our Sages (Nedarim 71a), Scripture says: \"If she should be to a man\" — concerning this woman who had imposed a prohibition upon herself in her father's house in her youth, and her vows are upon her — meaning that her father had not heard them, and they were neither annulled nor upheld, and her husband too heard them and annulled her vow, for he too participates with the father in the annulment of vows." "And the sense of <b>if she should be to a man</b> is: if she is betrothed to him while still in her father's house and has not yet come to his home — she is the betrothed (arusah), whom Scripture also calls \"his fellow's wife\" (Deuteronomy 22:24). And our Sages did not see fit to explain that the husband alone may annul, for his married wife (nesu'ah), the vows that she brought with her from her father's house into his home, as is manifest from the verse — for if so, what need is there for Scripture to go back and say, \"And if in her husband's house she vows\" (Numbers 30:11)? In the preceding cases he annuls; how much more so regarding vows in his own home! And the received tradition is decisive."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:37.336282722Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}